Check MACs in constant time, check the schemes, send real UKEY2 alerts

Three protocol details in the encrypted channel and the handshake:

- The HMAC-SHA256 over each SecureMessage is checked by comparing the
  computed tag with the received one using slice equality, which returns
  at the first differing byte. hmac's verify_slice compares in constant
  time.
- The SecureMessage header names its encryption and signature schemes;
  they are not checked, so a message that claims another scheme is
  decrypted as AES-256-CBC with HMAC-SHA256 all the same. Anything else
  is now refused.
- A UKEY2 alert is sent as a Ukey2Message whose message_type is the
  alert type instead of ALERT: BAD_MESSAGE_TYPE (2) goes out as a
  CLIENT_INIT, BAD_MESSAGE_DATA (4) as a CLIENT_FINISH, the others as
  undefined types, so the peer never reads an alert. It now goes out as
  type ALERT with the Ukey2Alert inside, as UKEY2 specifies.

Findings: none in the spec; hardening. Report: docs/UPSTREAM-QUICKSHARE.md R15.
